<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="5">
<Content>
<Paragraph Type="Scene Heading"><Text>INT. THE MAGNOLIA LOUNGE - NIGHT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Warm, loud, low-ceilinged. Christmas lights over the bar in July. A dance floor the size of a kitchen. A four-piece band on a bandstand one foot high.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pete on the door, calling every name coming in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Deena lands four longnecks on a table mid-story and keeps moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>JUNE comes in off the front step past Pete, tucking a pack into her apron pocket. Pete&#x27;s look follows her. She drops a basket at a table, then steps up onto the bandstand still in her apron and unclips it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(as she goes by)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>One of these days you&#x27;re gonna have to pick. Tray or that guitar.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I picked. This is the last tray.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Listen to her.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At a corner table, RAY BEASLEY, 70s, lean in a pressed shirt, holds his hat on one knee while ONDINE, 70s, in a floral dress, talks with both hands and straightens his collar.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the bar, a bottled Coke sweats before an empty stool. LACEY STROUD, 17, slight, bright red lipstick and a church dress taken in at the waist, slips in behind a birthday crowd and claims it.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>A man comes in alone. The only sport coat in the room. Pete holds the door for him and has no name to call. The man goes to the bar and stands at the back of the crowd with the wall behind him, where he can see all of it. This is MARTY KEEL.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Deena, going by with a tray, looks at him a second longer than a stranger gets. Keeps going.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>HOYT enters in a clean button-down, empty-handed. Pete&#x27;s handshake holds an extra beat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>You eat?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She on yet?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Pete lets go of his hand. Hoyt goes in.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Passing a rocking table, Hoyt crouches and folds a coaster beneath one leg. As he rises, Ondine catches him by the arm.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ONDINE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Merle ought to be ashamed. Nine years, Amos.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ain&#x27;t my place anymore.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>ONDINE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>It ain&#x27;t right, Amos.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>HOYT</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>I came for June.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>She lets him go. At the end of the bar, Deena sets a beer in front of him without being asked. The band finishes tuning. The drummer counts it in before June has the strap over her shoulder. The count dies. The room quiets.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>June looks once to the back of the room. A man she doesn&#x27;t know, at the bar, looking back. She doesn&#x27;t look there again.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>JUNE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(at the mic)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ray and Miss Ondine. Miss Ondine has talked me through every bad day I ever had, and Ray sat right there and let her. Thirty-one years married, tonight. This one&#x27;s theirs. And that man still won&#x27;t dance.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>RAY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(from his table)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Can&#x27;t. Not won&#x27;t.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>People at the tables laugh. Her boot comes down, one, two, and the band hits. A slow number.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Ondine looks at Ray. Ray holds his hat.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>June sings. Full voice now.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>The floor. Couples who have been here before, and two who haven&#x27;t, finding the step. A table by the wall stops talking in the middle of something. In the kitchen door, the kid in the wet apron with a bus tub on one hip, not moving.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Lacey on her stool, the Coke in both hands, watching the bandstand.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Hoyt at the end of the bar. The beer where Deena put it. He watches June.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the back of the crowd, Marty. He has stopped looking at the room and is looking at her. Deena works the bar in front of him, pulling longnecks for a tray.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>MARTY</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Does she know they&#x27;re here for her?</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Deena racks the bottles. Doesn&#x27;t answer. He waits, then gives it up and turns back to the floor. To June.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>DEENA</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(over his shoulder)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>She thinks they come because it&#x27;s Friday. She always has.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>He doesn&#x27;t turn around. He is smiling.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>At the door, Pete looks across the packed floor. People are still coming.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Character"><Text>BIG PETE</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Parenthetical"><Text>(half to himself)</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Dialogue"><Text>Ain&#x27;t even payday.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>Nobody answers. The floor fills.</Text></Paragraph>
<Paragraph Type="Action"><Text>June, eyes closed on the long note. Then open, on them. The couples. Ondine&#x27;s hand on Ray&#x27;s hat. The whole room facing one way.</Text></Paragraph>
</Content>
</FinalDraft>
